00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:07.550
Leah: Coding reviews are an integral part of the
software development process, but there are several

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:13.200
different ‘review methods’ that can used and
each has its own specific benefits and downsides

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.540
depending on your project and team structure.

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:19.909
Let’s look at some of the most common
methods for conducting coding reviews.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:25.123
Chris: Instant code review—also known
as pair programming title on screen

00:00:25.123 --> 00:00:30.970
In this type of review, there are two developers, one
will be on the keyboard producing the code and the other

00:00:31.049 --> 00:00:37.890
reviewing it as it is being written, paying attention to
potential issues and giving ideas for code improvement on

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.550
the go as well as identifying potential future problems.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:48.769
Leah: The developer typing is identified as the driver
and the developer reviewing the code is the navigator.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:53.119
When using this method, the two
programmers will switch roles frequently.

00:00:53.699 --> 00:00:59.730
By working together through the process of finding a
solution you increase the opportunity to get it right.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:06.810
The ‘driver’ is able to focus all their attention on
the task of coding knowing that the ‘navigator’ is there

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.440
to observe, give guidance and support when required.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.510
Chris: This type of review is useful when aiming for
fast high-quality code and a reduced cost of development.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:23.300
It is also useful for personal development, as the pair
can learn from each other and can help to improve morale.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:29.760
But there are also downsides to this type of review
as the pair need to work effectively together

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:36.560
as a team, and personal egos may impact on this
by one or other of the team feeling intimidated.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:41.920
You may also have the junior developer ‘driving’
and the more experienced developer ‘navigating’

00:01:42.389 --> 00:01:47.910
who may get frustrated that the driver is
too slow ultimately resulting in conflict.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:49.550
Leah: Inspection

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:50.644
Chris: title on screen

00:01:50.644 --> 00:01:54.750
Leah: This type of review is typically carried
out by peers, who are trained to review code by

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:57.850
looking at errors using well defined processes.

00:01:58.469 --> 00:02:04.959
It is commonly referred to as a Fagan inspection
after the inventor of the process, ‘Michael Fagan’.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.829
Chris: The principle behind this is that the
requirements are agreed at the beginning.

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:14.370
For each activity or operation, an
entry and exit criteria are specified.

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:20.589
The reviewer can then compare the results of the
activity to the pre-defined exit criteria, to

00:02:20.589 --> 00:02:23.589
validate that it has complied with these criteria.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.539
The steps followed in the inspection are.

00:02:26.539 --> 00:02:26.709
Bold titles on screen

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:33.600
Overview- the assignment of roles and the exit
requirements for each activity are established.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:40.179
Preparation- all those involved in the review prepare for
their roles, familiarise themselves with the requirements

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:46.890
for each section, inspect any supporting materials and
note down any questions or concerns that they may have.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:53.260
Inspection- this is when the actual review
takes place, and any errors are identified.

00:02:53.509 --> 00:02:58.209
Rework- developers correct any errors
that were found during the inspection.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:05.660
Follow up- a moderator checks over the rework to ensure
that all the identified errors have been corrected

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:09.160
and no new ones have been created during the fix.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:10.470
Walkthrough title on screen

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:15.930
Is a form of software peer review where the lead
designer or coder walks the members of the development

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:18.980
team and other stakeholders through the product.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.160
Those involved can ask questions or comment
on errors or other concerns they may have.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:30.949
This type of review is useful in that it gives
a broad overview of the product but is not as in

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:37.239
depth as other review types and lacks the direct
focus of on training and process development.

00:03:37.919 --> 00:03:39.379
Technical review title on screen

00:03:39.379 --> 00:03:45.500
This type of review is carried out by a team of
qualified developers who will undertake a peer review

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:51.850
of the product and look at it in detail, to ensure
that the product meets the suitability criteria for

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:58.150
its intended use, and any variance from the original
design and specification requirements that were agreed.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:03.190
The review team may also make
recommendations for alternative solutions.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:10.119
Technical reviews differ from inspections in their ability
to suggest direct alterations to the product reviewed,

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:14.590
rather than any focus on training or process improvement.

